The issue in the previous implementation was likely due to an off-by-one error or incorrect indexing when calculating the decreasing subsequence (specifically how the loop bounds interacted with the list slicing/negative indexing logic).

Here is the corrected, robust implementation. It calculates the Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) sum ending at each index (`inc[i]`) and the Longest Decreasing Subsequence (LDS) sum starting at each index (`dec[i]`). The maximum bitonic sum is then the maximum of `inc[i] + dec[i] - arr[i]` for all `i`.

```python
def max_sum(arr):
    n = len(arr)
    if n == 0:
        return 0

    # inc[i] stores the max sum of increasing subsequence ending at i
    inc = [0] * n
    # dec[i] stores the max sum of decreasing subsequence starting at i
    dec = [0] * n

    for i in range(n):
        inc[i] = arr[i]
        for j in range(i):
            if arr[j] < arr[i]:
                if inc[j] + arr[i] > inc[i]:
                    inc[i] = inc[j] + arr[i]

    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):
        dec[i] = arr[i]
        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):
            if arr[j] < arr[i]:
                if dec[j] + arr[i] > dec[i]:
                    dec[i] = dec[j] + arr[i]

    max_total = 0
    for i in range(n):
        # Sum of increasing part ending at i + decreasing part starting at i - arr[i] (counted twice)
        current_sum = inc[i] + dec[i] - arr[i]
        if current_sum > max_total:
            max_total = current_sum

    return max_total
```

